A||N||16||AusFiero||Need 2.8 performance tips. How to make it strong for a turbo||||5||
Z||000000||AusFiero||09-21-2001||08:36 AM||||Well I am sticking with my 2.8 for various reasons. Having a 35,000 mile engine being the main one. No I am going to turbo it. Do I need to do any beefing up of the bottom or top end of this engine to handle it. If so what do ya's recommend. Or could I just run say 9 pound boost and an intercooler and have reasonable performance without attacking the engine? Would this last on a stock engine? Or more to the point would the engine last? <P>Any advise from the turbo junkies would be appreciated here. I will be running it hooked up to the 5 speed out of my old GT. <P>Or scenario 2. Drop the 100,000 mile engine out of the old GT and rebuild it instead from the bottom up. Would this be better and what should  I do to it without spending a million bucks? I would still want to run a turbo.<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A><p>[This message has been edited by AusFiero (edited 09-21-2001).]||202.139.20.3||reg||5
Z||000001||Mr.Frosty||09-21-2001||10:30 AM||||All right,nice to see you stick with the 2.8 instead of an engine swap,and turbo too.I would keep the low mileage 2.8 for the future.Rebuild the other one with forged pistons,get an intercooler,a higher volume fuel pump,rising rate boost dependant fuel regulator,a blow off valve,and an oil cooler for the minimum.Down the road you will want to get a custom chip programmed for efficiency. Get the book "Maximum boost" by Corky bell.It is the best book on turbocharging.He knows more about the subject than anybody and I would listen to him before anyone else.Try to find a water cooled turbo for longevity.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mr.Frosty||09-21-2001||10:35 AM||||Oh yeah I almost forgot.You'll definately need a boost gauge and a air/fuel ratio gauge for the cockpit to keep things monitered.There is also a lot of cool stuff for a turbo fiero in the import aftermarket scene.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT Turbo intercooled||||reg||1
Z||000003||Neal||09-21-2001||10:45 AM||||9psi intercooled "should not" cause any excess wear on the motor.  9 is a low psi but the stock compression is high enough that i wouldnt try more boost if your concearned about the engine lasting.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Neal||09-21-2001||10:45 AM||||.<p>[This message has been edited by Neal (edited 09-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||c m west||09-21-2001||12:52 PM||||Also, it wouldnt hurt to install a breather system to the oil pan, thereby preventing blowing oil past the piston rings under mild boost||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||09-21-2001||04:22 PM||||“Blowing oil past the piston rings”. That’s a new one, ha ha ha ha ha.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||c m west||09-21-2001||05:05 PM||||Or don't and simply find out for your self||||reg||1
Z||000008||AusFiero||09-21-2001||05:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>Also, it wouldnt hurt to install a breather system to the oil pan, thereby preventing blowing oil past the piston rings under mild boost</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Can this happen? I need to know all the scenarios because building an engine in this country is not cheap. Any other safety precautions?<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||ds21||09-21-2001||05:44 PM||||Hey I was thinking about doing the same thing! So any info would help. Could some of you Turbo ppl give a ballpark figure on horsepower!||||reg||1
Z||000010||turbo86se||09-22-2001||11:40 AM||||Just to let you know, I've got a MillerWoods turbo on my 86SE. Installed around 30K miles, now with 55K. The 2.8 intself is completely stock. The turbo was purchased and installed from IRM many years ago. I'm running around 8-9lbs of boost, and have had zero problems with the turbo system itself. <P>I have had to replace the auto tranny once, and the front manifold cracked. I installed new manifolds, ported and polished, Jet Hot coated. Removed cat. convertor. <P>The boost is always there, and is a blast to drive. I love sitting at a red light, cars all around me, light turns green, hit the gas, front end lifts up, I go back into the seat, turbo guage says 4lbs boost, I hear the hissssssss, look in the rearview, not a car in sight, no blue lights either! What fun!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||09-23-2001||02:32 AM||||Good idea, especially if you   but I'd do this in any case. <P>Fordged pistons and rods are (IMO) a necessity for a turbo car.   As are better (more?) rings, and possibly a steel crankshaft (this costs bucks though  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>With 8 or 9 psi intercooled boost, and top notch electronics, you could maybe run an entirely stock 2.8, but I wouldn't expect to do so for 100,000miles.<P>I'd really suggest a totally aftermarket engine management system if you are up for it. You can patch up your way around it, but that generally leaves me more confused than doing it right the first time, and you will enjoy higher performance with the greater precision.<P>Should be one fun car when you're 'done' (a true artist is never done  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by c m west:<BR><B>Also, it wouldnt hurt to install a breather system to the oil pan, thereby preventing blowing oil past the piston rings under mild boost</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000012||87GTZ34||09-23-2001||10:47 AM||||Aus,  <P>In order to make that call, you need to spec the characteristics of the engine and how you plan to drive it.  Redline, desired powerband, max horsepower needed and type of driving/racing. If you plan on driving 'balls to the wall' all the time then even 9psi will need some additional beefing up.  Actually if you constantly pound on a stock eng w/o a turbo it won't handle it for very long.  If I were you (I'm doing a 3.4 DOHC blown): for the street with mild use, minimally I would have the crank deburred, use the early SBC (2 inch) performance rods, and balance it all, go high volume/pressure for the turbo.  For the street with heavier use and occasional strip, additionally have the crank cross-drilled for higher RPM oil flow and go to the forged pistons.  Also make sure your cooling system is fully operational and matched to your cooling needs (HP output, ambient temp, etc.)  Also, if you have to bore, go as little as possible to leave as much metal as possible for heat xfer under high load/high boost.<P>Eric is right, if you leave the stock PCV system in place, you will pressurize below the rings and defeat the mechanism that rings use for sealing.  i.e. The pressure differential of higher pressure on top and slight vacuum on the bottom cause the rings to expand with air pressure and ring tension.  The PCV valve plugs into the intake (at most 9lbs right) and if the valve leaks you have a pressure leak thru the crankcase.  The other end of the 'positive' system uses slight vacuum from in front of the TBI and this would also be at 9lbs w/ a turbo under full boost.  This will contribute to blow-by at the rings and on a blown engine, this is very undesirable.  <P>If you want info on the cross-drilling it is in the book Chevrolet POWER from HP books.  If you don't want to buy it, email me and I can give the details.  I would recommend it though as it is a good source of performance info <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||roadwarrior||09-23-2001||07:52 PM||||hey guys what would you say if I tried to turbo a 2.5? my buddy did it on his s10 and now has 100000km on it and hasn't done anything to it, I was thinking along the lines of finding a wrecked supra and ripping the sensors and and turbo off it or would I be better off to grab something off a voltswagon? this is just an idea so please give me your opignions||||reg||1
Z||000014||roadwarrior||09-23-2001||07:53 PM||||oh yeah and this engine will be purpose built cause I have a seized one in a parts car.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||gixxer||09-23-2001||09:13 PM||||Having an 88 V6 with a Design One system, I'll share a few things I have learned after 10 months.<BR>At the 9 lbs. of non-intercooled boost I've been running, I'd say the stock V6 is more than strong enough for casual street racing and twisty road running. No mods to my engine. The valve covers have never even been off.<BR>But, if you were to enter the realm of detonation for whatever reason..... <BR>An intercooler is a good idea and I'll probably put one on myself.<BR>No problems with the PCV system or pressurising the crankcase as my PCV fresh air intake is taken from a little K&N breathing ambient engine compartment air instead of the intake pipe going to the throttle body.<BR>Now my pet peeve;<BR>There is no way that the stock or altered ECM and PROM (WITH THE STOCK SIZE INJECTORS)is gonna give enough fuel to run the engine over, say 170 HP safely.<BR>With the stock 140 HP system's injectors running at 80% duty cycle already, you have only 20% more headroom for extra fuel. Above about 90% duty cycle you run the risk of the injectors going "static" and risk having some Almighty lean spots (bang).<BR>Seeing as how 9 lbs of boost will get you a 50% HP increase, running the injectors at even 100% duty cycle will still leave you with a substantial fuel shortfall. <BR>Here's what I did to my turbo setup on top of what Design One did(altered PROM).<BR>Tomco/Lucas 16 lb/hr injectors(up from 15).<BR>Walbro 255 lb/hr fuel pump.<BR>Cartech/BEGI rising rate FPR(set to 75 PSI, (a stock pump may deadhead as low as 60 PSI).<BR>An in-car Autometer electric fuel pressure guage.<BR>So I guess the moral of my story is;<BR>Think Fuel<BR>Get the fueling right, put a touch of timing retard in it, and have fun.<BR>And I second that opinon about Corky Bell's book. <BR>Avoid the book "Turbochargers" by Hugh MacInnes, waste of money.<BR>Good luck. <BR> <BR>     ||||reg||1
Z||000016||AusFiero||09-24-2001||03:46 AM||||Thanks guys. I am starting to get my head around some of the requirements now. I think I will by the book recommended twice and listen to my mechanic who obviously knows what he is talking about. I think I will beef up the engine in general though because I am a foot to the floor driver. <BR>Thanks again and will post pics along the way during the transformation.<BR><P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/ausfiero1.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A> | <A HREF="http://www.fieroshop.com.au" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Accessories</A>||202.139.20.3||reg||1
